x86_64-linux  there is nothing to compare here, by construction: the case includes <new>, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. The names that reach the runtime - _ZSt15set_new_handlerPFvvE, _ZSt15get_new_handlerv, _ZSt7nothrow, _ZnwmRKSt9nothrow_t, _ZnamRKSt9nothrow_t - agree with clang on arm64-darwin, one Itanium spelling serving both hosts; the Microsoft ones, ?set_new_handler@std@@YAP6AXXZP6AXXZ@Z and ??2@YAPEAX_KAEBUnothrow_t@std@@@Z and their twins, were measured from cl's own object and the case runs on the box.
x86_64-windows  there is nothing to compare here, by construction: the case includes <new>, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. The runtime names were measured from cl's own object (?set_new_handler@std@@YAP6AXXZP6AXXZ@Z, ?get_new_handler@std@@YAP6AXXZXZ, ?nothrow@std@@3Unothrow_t@1@B, ??2@YAPEAX_KAEBUnothrow_t@std@@@Z, ??_U@YAPEAX_KAEBUnothrow_t@std@@@Z) and the case runs on the box, linked against libcmt and libcpmt.
arm64-darwin  a difference about emission and not about names: cpp11 emits <new>'s inline constructors, the placement operators as the inline functions the header defines them as, its own vector-constructor loop and a memset where clang inlines, folds or calls bzero; and clang names the nothrow deletes, _ZdlPvRKSt9nothrow_t and _ZdaPvRKSt9nothrow_t, which are [expr.new]/20's cleanup for a constructor that throws inside a `new (std::nothrow)` expression - a cleanup cpp11 writes for no form of new, recorded as a gap and not a name - and an __Unwind_Resume for the same pad. Every name both compilers emit agrees.
